# No Inline JSX Handlers

This rule targets React/JSX code. Should be enforced by `no-restricted-syntax` in ESLint (blocks arrow functions with block bodies `{ }` in JSX props). `react/jsx-no-bind` still bans `.bind()` and `function` expressions.

## What Is Allowed

Single-expression arrow functions — no curly braces — are fine:

```tsx
<Card onClick={() => handleItemClick(item.id)} />
<Switch onCheckedChange={() => toggle(!value)} />
<Controller render={({ field }) => (<Input value={field.value} />)} />
```

## What Is Blocked

Arrow functions with a block body (`{ }`) in JSX props. These must be extracted into named functions at the component scope:

```tsx
// BAD — block body in JSX prop
<Sheet onOpenChange={open => {
  if (!open) {
    mutation.reset();
    setSheetState('none');
  }
}} />

// GOOD — extracted to a named function
const handleSheetClose = (open: boolean) => {
  if (!open) {
    mutation.reset();
    setSheetState('none');
  }
};

<Sheet onOpenChange={handleSheetClose} />
```

## Naming Convention

Name handlers descriptively using the `handle` prefix: `handlePrevClick`, `handleNameChange`, `handleFormSubmit`. When a handler needs arguments from a loop, define the outer function and call it from a thin wrapper:

```tsx
const handleItemClick = (itemId: string) => {
  setSelected(itemId);
  navigate(`/items/${itemId}`);
};

{items.map((item) => (
  <Card key={item.id} onClick={() => handleItemClick(item.id)} />
))}
```
